steps.explanation:
- $V_s$ alone ($I_s$ open): $v' = V_s\frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2}$ = {{v1|V}}
- $I_s$ alone ($V_s$ shorted): $v'' = I_s(R_1 \parallel R_2)$ = {{v2|V}}
$v = v' + v''$ = **{{v|V}}**
Check with one node equation: $v = \frac{V_s/R_1 + I_s}{1/R_1 + 1/R_2}$ gives the same.

- $V_{s1}$ alone ($V_{s2}$ shorted, so $R_2 \parallel R_3$): $v' = V_{s1}\frac{R_2 \parallel R_3}{R_1 + R_2 \parallel R_3}$ = {{va|V}}
- $V_{s2}$ alone ($V_{s1}$ shorted, so $R_1 \parallel R_3$): $v'' = V_{s2}\frac{R_1 \parallel R_3}{R_2 + R_1 \parallel R_3}$ = {{vb|V}}
$v = v' + v''$ = **{{v|V}}**

Each side is a voltage divider measured from the bottom wire:
$v_a = v_s\frac{R_3}{R_1 + R_3}$ = {{va|V}}, $v_b = v_s\frac{R_x}{R_2 + R_x}$ = {{vb|V}}
$v_{ab} = v_a - v_b$ = **{{vab|V}}**

- $V_s$ alone ($I_s$ open): $R_2 \parallel (R_3 + R_4)$ = {{Rr|Ω}}, so the left node is at $V_s\frac{R_2 \parallel (R_3 + R_4)}{R_1 + R_2 \parallel (R_3 + R_4)}$ = {{v1a|V}} and $i_4' = \frac{ {{v1a|V}} }{R_3 + R_4}$ = {{i4a|A}}
- $I_s$ alone ($V_s$ shorted): $i_4''$ = {{i4b|A}} (current divider)
$i_4 = i_4' + i_4''$ = **{{i4|A}}**. A two-node analysis gives the same result.

Replace the bridge by its Thévenin equivalent seen from a–b:
- $V_t = v_{ab} = v_s\left(\frac{R_3}{R_1 + R_3} - \frac{R_x}{R_2 + R_x}\right)$ = {{va|V}} − {{vb|V}} = {{vab|V}}
- $R_t = R_1 \parallel R_3 + R_2 \parallel R_x$ = {{Rt|Ω}}
$i_g = \frac{V_t}{R_t + R_g}$ = {{vab|V}} / {{Rt + Rg|Ω}} = **{{vab/(Rt + Rg)|A}}**
